<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Renault</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Renault is a French car maker founded in 1899 by Louis Renault and his brothers at Boulogne-Billancourt near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It built taxis, lorries and tanks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After Louis Renault was accused of collaborating with the German occupiers, the company was nationalised in 1945 as the Régie Nationale des Usines Renault, and it was privatised in 1996.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company was founded on 25 February 1899 as Société Renault Frères by Louis Renault and his brothers Marcel and Fernand. Louis, a young engineer, had already designed and built several prototypes. He handled design and production, while Marcel and Fernand, who had gained their business skills in their father's textile firm, managed the business. The first Renault car, the Voiturette 1CV, was sold to a friend of Louis' father after a test ride on 24 December 1898. Louis also invented a direct drive gear with no drive belt, which gave much better performance uphill. Marcel Renault's racing interests took the firm into motor sport early: he won the 1902 Paris-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> rally but lost his life in the Paris-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> rally of 1903.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After 1918 Louis Renault widened the firm's scope to agricultural and industrial machinery. The war of 1914 to 1918 had led to many new products, and the first Renault tractor, the Type GP, built from 1919 to 1930, was based on the FT tank. The company struggled to compete with increasingly popular small, affordable cars, and in 1920 Louis signed one of its first distribution contracts with Gustave Gueudet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>.</p><p>After the French capitulation in 1940 German forces took control of the Renault factories. On 3 March 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> sent 235 low-level bombers against the plant on the Île Seguin at Billancourt, the largest number aimed at a single target during the war; 460 metric tons of bombs fell on the plant and its surroundings, with heavy civilian casualties. American bombing followed on 4 April 1943, and the plant was hit again in September of that year. Under Pierre Lefaucheux after the war the company recovered commercially but met labour unrest that continued into the 1980s.</p><p>The Renault 5 of January 1972 anticipated the energy crisis of 1973, and the Espace of 1984 was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first multi-purpose vehicle. In 1984 the company was losing a billion francs a month, and the government installed Georges Besse as chairman; he sold non-core assets and cut staff, but was murdered by the group Action Directe in November 1986. His successor Raymond Lévy sold American Motors to Chrysler in 1987. The state-owned company was privatised in 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Renault has been prominent in motor sport. It won the 24 Hours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a> in 1978 with the Alpine A442 and introduced the turbo engine to Formula One with the RS01 at Silverstone in 1977. Its early work on mathematical curve modelling for car bodies is significant in the history of computer graphics. It is headquartered at Boulogne-Billancourt, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and has belonged to an alliance with Nissan since 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Renault” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It nationalised <b>Renault</b>, coal, electricity and major banks, created the social security system and the Monnet Plan for reconstruction, and oversaw the trials of collaborators.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-french-republic">Provisional Government of the French Republic</a></li></ul></section>
